SUMMARY:Lecture: Weird and Wonderful Animals on Etruscan Mirrors
LOCATION:120 W Gasparilla Plaza Tampa \, FL 33602 United States
DESCRIPTION:Etruscan mirrors (most date to ca. 500-200 BCE) are an importa
 nt source for depictions of animals and their interactions with humans\, e
 specially women. Animals in the ancient world\, whether real\, fantastic\,
  domesticated or wild\, can inform us about many aspects of ancient life\,
  whether domestic life\, religion\, or the zoological world of the ancient
  imagination. But in Etruscan art animals often show up in surprising and 
 unexpected ways\, especially in mythic and religious contexts. For instanc
 e\, the winged Lasa\, a uniquely Etruscan divinity\, is sometimes depicted
  with a deer. And although often ignored in the scholarship\, interesting 
 animals sometimes appear in contexts of divination or prophecy\, such as b
 irth and wedding scenes. Were the animals simply filler for the scene\, or
  did they carry some deeper meaning or purpose? This talk will examine som
 e of these weird and wonderful animals and their association with humans\,
  especially women\, in a variety of mirror scenes for what they may tell u
 s about Etruscan culture. Lectures are offered free with the cost of daily
  admission: Art Museum Members: Free Not-Yet-Members: 25 College Students:
  Free \n\nPermalink: https://www.tampa.gov/node/197216
